+## September 15 UTC reusable-card review (PR #23)
+
+**Confirmed requirement:** Greg requested another thorough review because the physical card system will be reused across several levels. Review baseline is `17a3253921334733581fae9b361e796ed861a36e`; work stays on `feature/keycard-reader-light-feedback`. The previous sticky-hand/forcefield/unreliable-grab report remains failed runtime validation, and the historical one-card 1/2 result does not validate this revision.
+
+**Factual correction:** the installed XR Interaction Toolkit is **2.6.4** in both `Packages/manifest.json` and `Packages/packages-lock.json`. Prior review/PR wording saying 2.5.4 was incorrect and is superseded. Unity remains **2022.3.55f1** and Photon PUN is unchanged.
+
+**Implemented corrections:** all public physical-card acceptance now runs through one single-use transaction; consumption is committed before callbacks, preventing a listener or second public box entry from crediting the same card twice. An authored matching reader protects its lock in every level, not just the Level 1 travel lock. New Inspector-authored boxes default to requiring readers, with explicit compatibility for unbound legacy direct boxes. Reader authorization verifies its exact objective, the current explicit credential, local-held eligibility and actual root solid-collider overlap; accessory colliders and disabled Rigidbody collision cannot scan. Explicit null binding stays unbound, negative authored lookup is cached, rebinds invalidate old acknowledgement state, and re-enable/rebind rescans nearby solid candidates to recover sleeping-card overlaps.
+
+**Implemented pickup/recovery corrections:** new/legacy physical cards acquire held-collision safety; collider fitting excludes VFX and nested other cards; runtime-added cards refresh XRI's collider-to-interactable map. The two imported active cards serialized Instantaneous movement, contrary to the intended held-world collision behavior; `KeyCard` now normalizes to VelocityTracking while retaining lightweight continuous/interpolated physics. Release waits for XRI teardown, solid rig separation and both virtual hand query volumes; whole-object reactivation preserves protection. Fall recovery retains its temporary grab-disable ownership across deactivation, wakes loose cards to settle, and does not restore consumed cards. Panel subscription uses the actual bound source, ignores stale in-flight event arguments, respects explicit unbinding and never shows all-green progress merely because its lamp array is too small.
+
+**Implemented authoring/test support:** `CardSystemValidator` provides read-only loaded-scene and selected-prefab/hierarchy menus; `docs/keycard-system-reuse.md` records cross-level setup, supported lifecycle and acceptance gates. A .NET harness compiles actual `CardConsumptionState` production code rather than a Python translation. Unity Play Mode fixtures cover credential matrix, public API/cross-lock/reentrant consumption, binding, collider mapping, disable/re-enable, recovery, sleeping cards and physical settling; their engine execution remains pending. The source parser now includes those fixture/editor sources. Existing negative source fixtures and coordinate-math checks remain separate from engine tests.
+
+**Reuse boundary / unchanged design:** these remain personal, visit-local, consumed objective cards, not pooled reusable passes or shared Photon props. Mixed-credential quotas, persistent cross-level reward cards and non-consumed passes remain separate undecided/unimplemented design work. A multi-reader lock counts distinct accepted cards, not one quota per credential. Level 1 stays two Amber cards with the existing travel rules. The panel prefab still has four slots; larger requirements need more authored lamps. Fall recovery must be tuned below the lowest playable elevation of each new level. No scene geometry, global layer matrix, input binding, Crawler behavior or networking architecture is changed.
+
+**Pending validation:** run the new managed harness and complete source suite against the reviewed source, then run Unity import/compile, the new Play Mode fixture, read-only authoring validation, both existing Editor validators, real repeated card/hand/wall tests, full completion/travel/recovery, independent two-client Photon and Quest/headset checks. VelocityTracking responsiveness and wall behavior require hands-on validation. Source checks do not prove a perfect or runtime-ready system.
